<a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
</div>
<h2 id="injury">What Happened</h2>
<p>Williams went down in the fourth quarter of Sunday's loss to Minnesota after coming up lame on a scramble — a non-contact injury, the kind that typically points to a soft-tissue strain rather than structural damage. He was emotional while being carted off but was reportedly seen walking with only a slight limp in the locker room afterward. An MRI on Monday confirmed the team's cautious optimism: no major tear, but a hamstring strain that Ben Johnson is calling "week-to-week" rather than putting a firm number of games on it.</p>
<div class="stat-card">
<div class="stat-icon">🩹</div>
<div class="stat-title">Caleb Williams — Injury Snapshot</div>
<div class="stat-sub">Week 2 vs. Minnesota Vikings · Non-Contact</div>
<div class="stat-numbers">
<div class="stat-num"><div class="num">0</div><div class="label">Games Missed, Career</div></div>
<div class="stat-num"><div class="num">2-0</div><div class="label">Bears Record</div></div>
<div class="stat-num"><div class="num">Week-to-Week</div><div class="label">Official Status</div></div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Tyson Bagent finished the game in relief and would start in Williams' place if he can't go Monday against Philadelphia. It would be the first missed game of Williams' NFL career — he's played through his first three seasons without an absence, a streak that stands out given how frequently his playing style puts him in position to get hurt.</p>
<h2 id="risk">Why Mobile QBs Carry This Risk</h2>
<p>Hamstring strains are an overstriding injury — they happen when the leg reaches for extra ground at high speed, which is exactly the mechanic a scrambling quarterback repeats dozens of times a game. Williams finished last season top-11 among all quarterbacks in both rush attempts and rush yards, extending plays and picking up yardage with his legs in a way pocket passers simply don't. That ability is a huge part of what makes his offense so hard to defend — and it's also repeated exposure to the exact motion that causes hamstring strains.</p>
<p>This isn't a new pattern in the league. Lamar Jackson dealt with a hamstring injury of his own in 2025 and is frequently cited as a blueprint for how a dual-threat quarterback manages this kind of recovery: conservative early-week rest, a full workload return only once speed and change of direction are back to normal, and a willingness to sit out a game or two rather than re-aggravate it. Soft-tissue injuries that return too early tend to linger and recur — which is likely why the Bears are deliberately avoiding a hard timeline on Williams' return.</p>
