<h2 id="outlook">Williams' Outlook for Week 3</h2>
<p>The Bears aren't ruling Williams out for Monday night against the Eagles, but he isn't expected to practice this week, and the team plans to lean on updates from its training staff before deciding. Given how fragile hamstring injuries can be — and how much of Williams' game depends on the exact motion that causes them — a cautious approach protects both this week and the rest of the season. If he can't go, Bagent inherits a 2-0 offense but without the scrambling threat that has defined it.</p>
<div class="callout">
<strong>The Trade-Off</strong>
  Williams' rushing ability is a real weapon — but every dual-threat quarterback who leans on his legs as heavily as Williams does takes on more soft-tissue injury risk than a pure pocket passer. It's not a reason to change his game; it's just the cost of the version of quarterback play that makes him so difficult to defend in the first place.
</div>
